you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  too hot to fly the fire fighting planes  evidence of the extreme conditions we'll  increasingly face  with wildfires across southern europe  and in the united states and south  america  more heat waves more droughts more  cyclones a stark warning for the future  of  india but is the world's third biggest  emitter  ready to play its part at cop26  and if we can do it why can't everyone  the island of northern ireland that aims  to be  self-sufficient in renewable energy by  the end of the decade  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  the warning from the intergovernmental  panel on climate change was stark and  shocking and we start today with  evidence of just how severe the impact  of climate change is  right now well in a moment we'll look at  what it could mean  for india but first to the increasingly  disturbing pattern  of extreme wildfires across the world  and whilst there have always been  wildfires we know from the ipcc report  that more extreme  extensive and destructive fires are the  harsh reality of climate change  well wildfires in parts of greece  including evia island near athens have  been burning for days now and there are  increasing concerns about fires in  puglia calabria and sicily  in italy there's also the huge  dixie fire in california and now  fast-growing fires in several countries  in south america including  peru and bolivia meanwhile wildfires are  still raging in parts of siberia in  russia  and four people have died in wildfires  in algeria  well this is the scene from bolivia  where fires have swept across the  eastern lowlands in the department of  santa cruz an area known for its rich  wildlife  this year forest fires have consumed  more than 360 thousand acres in the area  and in peru fires have started in the  city of cushco in the andes  air force helicopters have been called  in to drop water over the fires that  started on thursday  in russia there have been 155 forest  fires across  a large area of siberia two villages  have been evacuated  and dozens more are threatened while  buildings and houses have been  destroyed in greece the prime minister  kiriakos mitsutakis has apologized for  failures in tackling the wildfires  that have burned across the country for  days and approved a further  500 million euros for relief and  compensation  well sky's michelle clifford has the  latest from evia the hard-hit island  near athens  and michelle the prime minister there  has called this a natural disaster of  unprecedented dimensions  how are attempts to control the fires  they're now faring  they're still really difficult to weak  on the fires are still blazing  uh in greece and on here on the island  of  evia we hear a constant stream of  firefighting  helicopters going overhead trying to  douse the land uh with water but there  are incredibly high temperatures  today and that's made the task all the  harder uh firefighters from  other european nations and further  afield have now come  to greece to help with the effort but as  you mentioned  the uh greek prime minister has gone on  television and he's apologized  for some of the government failings in  terms of the response he also talked  about  a lack of being prepared for such a  catastrophic situation and he issued  a warning saying that the climate crisis  is knocking on the door  of the entire planet and i suppose anna  that  is a reminder of what experts have been  warning off for a very long time  that what we've seen in europe over  recent weeks be it flooding  or be it wildfires extremes of weather  and temperature  are going to be more frequent in the  future and the warning is  that governments and leaders really need  to be prepared  mitchell clifford thank you well let's  turn out to india  crucial in the story of climate change  yesterday's ipcc report  said the indian subcontinent will face  far more extreme weather  and is likely to suffer from more severe  heat stress and flooding  soon to overtake china as the most  populous nation india is also the third  biggest emitter on the planet so must  play a significant role if the world is  to tackle the crisis  well the ipcc report says india will  face more  heat waves and humid heat stress will be  more intense and frequent  in this century than the last the report  also says  that the decrease in rainfall from the  south and southeast monsoon in  recent decades is due to human activity  but that in the long term  the country will face more heavy monsoon  rainfall  now the indian ocean is heating faster  than other areas and the sea level is  rising  at 3.7 millimeters every year now that  means extreme sea level events that used  to take place once a century  will now happen nearly every year but  india is not just a victim of climate  change  it's a major emitter and its future  emissions will be a hotly contested part  of any deal at cop26 now  china overtook the us to become the  world's biggest emitter  in 2006. so india is now in  third place and responsible for 7  of the world's co2 emissions india  says western developed nations must  shoulder the greatest burden in reducing  emissions and argues  the ipcc report vindicates its position  well let's get a sense of the mood in  delhi a crucial player at cop 26  our indie correspondent neville lazarus  is in the indian capital  so neville what is the reaction to the  ipcc report there in india  has that its stark warning had an impact  on the country's willingness to  to make changes at cop26 the report  hasn't  has been talked about in the newspapers  in in television studios on the news but  that's where it ends there hasn't been  much of reaction from the government in  fact there's been very slow inclination  of the speed and scope of of  the efforts from the government in terms  of mitigating  uh climate change we've seen we just  moved from one catastrophe to the next  it's one  cyclone to the to the flooding or or to  a massive heat wave or glacial  uh sort of breaches which have killed  hundreds of people and so  uh there hasn't been much effort from  the government to show  or or to talk about it it it we just  move on from one  one uh one uh one catastroph trophy to  the to the next and  we do have now a new minister who's uh  who came in last month he's an enviro  he's he's a lawyer he's uh he's an  environmentalist he's uh  he's co authored a book on environment  uh he has talked about decoupling  emissions  and uh and and economic progress  and we hope that there will be a change  but it has to be  has to be done really really fast and  the scope has to be  uh has to be across this country because  we're dealing with the country which is  almost 2000  miles from north to south and east west  with a really  large and long coastal uh  coastal belts and coastal region which  uh which  where there are millions of people  living and and each  each month they are being battered by  some sort of climate change  and environmental hazard never lazarus  thanks very much indeed  in today's other climate news 84 of  people in wales believe they need to  make substantial lifestyle changes to  address climate change  that's the finding of a new survey by  the welsh government that showed only 15  of respondents thought climate change  will affect their local area a great  deal  that's in stark contrast to the latest  ipcc report  which warns that rising sea levels will  affect coastlines in wales and lead to  the significant relocation of  communities there  just nine percent of small firms measure  their carbon footprint  falling to just five percent for micro  businesses  a new survey of members by the british  chambers of commerce  shows many smaller companies are  struggling to get to grips with their  own carbon emissions  a new service has been launched with o2  to help smaller firms get information  about transitioning  to net zero and life-sized  lion sculptures have appeared in london  as part of a global art installation  launched in support of conservation  across the african continent  the sculptures aim to highlight the  threats faced by lions such as climate  change  while raising funds for community  conservation climate change is driving  habitat loss  and increasing droughts in countries  like kenya and botswana  leading to conflict between humans and  lions  wrestling island six miles off the coast  of northern ireland  wasn't connected to the electricity grid  until 2007  but aims to produce its own wind and  wave energy  by the end of the decade residents say  if it can be achieved at this remote  location on the end of the grid  it can be achieved anywhere ahead of the  target our senior island correspondent  david blevins reports from ratlin island  it's the most northern part of northern  ireland  and it's easy to see why wrathlyn is  concerned about the climate crisis  the island wasn't connected to the grid  until 2007  but aims to be carbon neutral by the end  of this decade  there's great possibilities now with  hydrogen fueled ferries and  and we can produce hydrogen on our often  so i mean that  achieves two goals it reduces our carbon  footprint hopefully to zero but it also  gives us some security and that we're  generating  our own energy and retailing our own  energy  kate burns is an underwater farmer she's  battling climate change  by harnessing the potential in seaweed  these seeds are swimming in kelp  and it's as rich in protein as beef  there is as much protein in kelp and  also  it's higher in calcium and iron than any  other vegetable including spinach  and you know the thing about kelp is you  grow it in the sea  you don't need any fertilizers or any  pesticides or any herbicides  in fact the more you grow the better it  is for the environment  tom's passion for wildlife photography  has taken him around the world  but he keeps coming home to rafflin and  wants to see his subjects protected  there has to be a change at some stage  and we need to do it  sooner rather than later because i mean  you just see with the news or the fires  in the world and the heating up of  things  possibly see temperatures rising you  know it's not looking too good at the  moment  you can't live in a place like this and  not appreciate the environment  but islanders want others to share their  aspiration for carbon neutrality  they say if it can be achieved here  at the end of the grid it can be  achieved anywhere  residents are deeply conscious that they  share this island  and the waters around it and are  fighting for those who can't fight for  themselves  david blevins sky news on wrathlon  island  rewilding is a word we hear more and  more it's a simple concept but could  play a crucial part in tackling both  climate change  and the biodiversity crisis we now face  a new four-year project has just been  launched to discover the best way to  restore woodland and  meadow habitats in the uk  [Music]  well that's everything from us for today  coming up on tomorrow's show why climate  change is putting the ancient  world at risk of disappearing you can  catch that at the same time here on sky  news  see you then

